<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zabeau Bellanton</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1751–after 1782</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Elisabeth Bellanton</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zabeau Bellanton was a slave trader in the French colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> and its most successful businesswoman before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>. A free woman of colour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cap-haitien">Cap-Haïtien</a>, officially listed as a jam maker, she built her business herself rather than on money left by a white lover, as most such women had. She bought the cheapest enslaved Africans straight from the slave ships, the young and the sick, pawned them out for some months until they were fit to sell, then sold them, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> she also bought from smuggling British ships. The method was thought immoral even in the colony, but it made her rich in town houses, and in 1782 she left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p></div>
